If n is such that 36 ≤ n ≤ 72, then x = (n2 + 2√n(n + 4) + 16) / (n+ 4√n+ 4) satisfies Answer Choices: (A) 20 < x < 54 (B) 23 < x < 58 (C) 25 < x < 64 (D) 28 < x < 60 (E) None of these
A nice algebra question!

Let's simplify the expression: \begin{align*}
x &= \frac{n^2 + 2\sqrt{n(n+4)} + 16}{n+4\sqrt{n}+4} \\
&= \frac{(n+2)^2}{n+4\sqrt{n}+4} \quad (\text{complete the square}) \\
&= 2 - \frac{4n-8}{n+4\sqrt{n}+4}
\end{align*}Now, we are given that $36 \leq n \leq 72$, so $4 \sqrt{n} \leq 12$, which means $n + 4\sqrt{n} + 4 \geq 12$. This gives \begin{align*}
x &= 2 - \frac{4n-8}{n+4\sqrt{n}+4} \\
&\leq 2 - \frac{4n-8}{12} \\
&= \frac{16-4n}{12} \\
&< 2.
\end{align*}The only answer that is correct is (C) 25 < x < 64.
Therefore, among A through E, the answer is \textbf{C}

